from common import *
R=[('collapsed/intersecting edges on concave shapes (MFCG 87882877)','snap rounding, attraction and the clearance invariant; cuts are splitFace transactions with stated contact semantics, validated before commit (§3.6, §3.10).'),
('silent no-op of the 2017 cut routine when a cut did not make exactly two crossings, and its unbounded recursion (os-model.md §13.4)','E_NOT_TWO_CROSSINGS under exact contact rules - vertex grazes, overlaps and antennae defined, slits are connectHole (§3.6); recursion bounded by the class band and a depth cap, every rejection logged (§4.6).'),
('null OBB / rect crash (61026228)','one OBB contract (§3.1); snap rejects NaN, +-Infinity and out-of-range values in every build; computed divisors go through ratio() (§3.2 (7)-(8)).'),
('"two intersections with the same world position" (FTG 153121107)','exactly one vertex per lattice point; points within attractCm (26 cm) of a vertex or edge are attracted onto it; near-coincident stretches are one shared edge with merged roles (§3.6).'),
('stale graph state after edits breaking district re-roll (FTG 153121107)','no record outside the kernel stores an arena slot (§2.2 rule 5).'),
('hourglass/sliver footprints (FTG 133016301)','the invariant suite - simple faces, clearance, class floors in width and area (§3.10); frontage-first construction (§4.7); sliver absorption that never deletes a street, wall or shore edge (§3.6).'),
('self-intersecting user strokes breaking the server (FTG 2026-03)','insertChain decimates to minEdge, splits self-touching input into simple runs and validates at the authoring boundary (§3.6).'),
('NG 1.2.2 silent rotated-frame retries and its hole-aware bisector two logged loops (a balance retry up to 20 in random frames then accepted; a restart when no far edge exists) (NG RA-3; P7)','the bent cut declared ladder (§4.6 step 8), each rung under an operation-count work limit (§3.3): its first rung re-plans in a turned frame a bounded number of times, and no rung restarts anything above the face.'),
('boolean "thorns" (VG 50688261)','booleans and buffers on the kernel own arrangement, under the same attraction, minEdge and clearance as every chain, with no re-snap round trip (§3.7).'),
('"weird steps" of per-edge buffers and "not so much concave ones" for shrinking (2017 author, os-geometry.md §2.1.4)','one kerb function, committed by corridorUnion; the half-plane inset only for uniform convex insets (§3.7).'),
('skeleton "glitches in special cases" (MFCG 101173551)','a stated numeric contract, exact OBB roofs for convex near-rectangles, a validator on every output and typed downgrades (§3.8).'),
('identity-keyed topology, un-serialisable state (OS §1.1)','indexed arenas; each arena - one per PlatKey - serialised once (§3.9).'),
('allocation hot spot of NG 1.2.2 in creating points and intersecting lines (its profile, G-R7)','a struct-of-arrays kernel with allocation-free intersection and orientation routines (§3.1), measured in M0.')]
n=224
for a,b in R:
    it(f'KERNEL-{n:04d}','traceability','3.5',f'Creator failure class closed: {a} -> kernel answer: {b}',['§3.5 table'],'kernel (summary; each answer normative at its cited home)',[],['§15 improvement matrix','§13 tests of the cited mechanisms'],['failure class'],summary_of=True); n+=1
# n = 237
it('KERNEL-0237','mechanism','3.6','Attraction (GEO-3; J-K2): insertChain processes each chain of a batch in the batch order (canonical for a declared op set, issue order in a callback-form stage, §3.3) in five steps. Its CANDIDATES are: (a) the vertices and edges of level <= the batch level; (b) the finer vertices lying on those edges routes where the chain ends on or joins such a route (a neighbour plot-line end on the kerb where II.1 (3) neighbour step lands its corridor_split edge; a close-up kept fabric beside a new junction); (c) in a batch declaring a clear region, its prepared vertices (KernelTx.prepared, §3.3 rules 2-3). Nothing finer is a candidate.',['§3.6 Attraction'],'kernel',['@insertChain','@prepared','II.1 (3)'],['@insertChain steps 1-5','§4 every chain insertion'],['attraction','candidates (a) (b) (c)','GEO-3','J-K2'])
it('KERNEL-0238','rule','3.6','Promotion by attraction: a finer vertex of (b) or (c) that a chain vertex lands on, or a chain segment is routed through, is promoted to the batch level (§2.2 rule 3; W_PROMOTED, listed in the commit record), its finer edges kept. A (c) promotion clears the vertex split flag (it is a vertex of the new coarse chain) and strips an anchor contact name (its work done); a (b) promotion keeps the flag (the vertex still lies on the edge it split), and removing the promoting chain later returns it to its level by demotion (§2.2 rule 3; J-K28). A clear-region batch never shares a finer edge (§3.3 rule 2).',['§3.6 Attraction'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 3'],['W_PROMOTED','@CommitRecord promoted/unflagged','@removeEdges demotion'],['promotion','split flag','J-K28'])
it('KERNEL-0239','rule','3.6','Obstacles: kept geometry, and inside a clear region every finer vertex and edge other than the prepared contacts, is an obstacle: a chain crossing it or coming within attractCm of it returns E_KEPT or E_LEVEL (§3.3). A chain is inserted run by run; the part already inserted counts as existing geometry for later parts, so a chain doubling back near itself attracts onto itself like any other edge.',['§3.6 Attraction'],'kernel',['@keptContent','@clearRegion'],['@E_KEPT','@E_LEVEL'],['self-attraction','obstacle'])
it('KERNEL-0240','step','3.6','insertChain step 1 Snap and decimate: every point passes snap (§3.2 (7)); consecutive points closer than minEdge are decimated - the later dropped unless its index is in opts.pinned or it is a knot (§3.4); two such protected points closer than minEdge merge at the first, reported, and a merged knot vertex carries both ordinals (a step in its profile). A chain that touches or crosses itself is split into simple runs.',['§3.6 step 1','§3.5 row self-intersecting strokes'],'kernel',['@snap','@minEdge','opts.pinned','opts.knots'],['§7.1 user strokes','§2.2 rule 5 profile step'],['decimation','simple runs'])
it('KERNEL-0241','step','3.6','insertChain step 2 Vertex attraction: a chain vertex within attractCm of an existing vertex moves onto it (nearest; ties to the smaller lattice position, y then x). Otherwise, within attractCm of an existing edge (nearest; ties by the edges canonical keys, §3.3), take the exact foot on that edge: if the foot lies within attractCm of one of the edge endpoints, the vertex moves onto that endpoint (the nearer; ties y then x), because a split there would leave a sub-edge shorter than minEdge; otherwise it moves onto the edge at the foot placement point by splitThrough - a Steiner vertex if the edge is coarser. Worked example: vertex (20, 20) beside edge (0,0)-(1 000,0): 28.3 cm from (0,0) (not vertex-attracted), 20 cm from the edge, foot (20,0) 20 cm from (0,0), so it moves onto (0,0). Chain vertices attraction brings within minEdge of each other merge, the later into the earlier, as in step 1.',['§3.6 step 2','§3.2 (2)'],'kernel',['@attractCm','@placement','@splitThrough'],['W_ATTRACTED'],['vertex attraction','exact foot'])
it('KERNEL-0242','step','3.6','insertChain step 3 Segment and stretch attraction: a chain segment passing within attractCm of an existing vertex is routed through it. Where a segment runs within attractCm of an existing edge over a stretch (near-parallel approach, or a crossing shallower than minFaceAngle) the stretch is shared: the chain joins the edge where the stretch begins, follows it and leaves where it ends, the shared edges gain the chain roles (W_STRETCH_SHARED). A join or leave point within attractCm of an endpoint of the edge moves onto that endpoint (as step 2); any other join or leave point is placed by the placement rule; a stretch whose join and leave points would lie closer than minEdge collapses to one contact point, handled by step 2.',['§3.6 step 3'],'kernel',['@placement'],['W_STRETCH_SHARED'],['stretch sharing','routed through'])
it('KERNEL-0243','step','3.6','insertChain step 4 Crossings: remaining crossings computed exactly, placed by the placement rule and resolved by iterated snap rounding (<= 3 passes; each pass leaves a planar arrangement, so an unconverged result still commits, reported W_SNAP_PASSES; sub-centimetre re-routes of Steiner vertices are snap-rounding re-routes with the same guarantee).',['§3.6 step 4'],'kernel',['@placement'],['W_SNAP_PASSES'],['snapRounding','iterated snap rounding','3 passes'])
it('KERNEL-0244','rule','3.6','Wedge routing (step 4): two consecutive crossings closer than minEdge lie on the two arms of an acute corner (the clearance invariant forbids any other way) and are resolved at that corner instead of cutting a sliver off its tip (W_WEDGE_ROUTED): when the corner is the existing arrangement (two crossings along one new segment) the chain is routed through the corner vertex; when it is the chain own (two crossings along one existing edge) the chain corner vertex moves onto that edge at the placement point of its foot. The chain moves at most minEdge / (2 tan(theta/2)) for a corner of angle theta: 0.47 m at the 30 deg junction floor, 1.2 m at the 12 deg plot floor. An optional chain that would move more than attractCm this way is rejected with E_BELOW_MIN_EDGE.',['§3.6 step 4','§3.6 existing geometry wins (d)'],'kernel',['@invariants clearance'],['W_WEDGE_ROUTED','@E_BELOW_MIN_EDGE'],['wedge routing','corner bound','30 deg junction floor'])
it('KERNEL-0245','step','3.6','insertChain step 5 Re-walk: only faces incident to touched edges are re-walked; face records, owners, parents and stage ranks are updated - a kept face is re-parented into the new face that contains it (§3.3); the commit record lists them.',['§3.6 step 5','§3.3 kept content'],'kernel',['@CommitRecord'],['@reparent','@invariants'],['re-walk'])
it('KERNEL-0246','mechanism','3.6','The placement rule (GEO-3, GEO-35; J-K17): every new vertex on an existing edge (a split foot, a stretch join or leave point, a crossing) is placed at the lattice point nearest its exact position among points that (i) lie within 0.71 cm of the existing edge route at the edge own level (one index item, §3.3), so that edge moves no more than its hot pixel; (ii) lie at least minEdge from the endpoints of the edge being split and, for a crossing, from the endpoints of the crossing segment; (iii) keep every vertex and edge around the split at least minEdge from the new vertex and from every edge incident to it (clearance, checked in the index); ties y then x. The search runs outward from the exact position, never beyond attractCm, charging each candidate to the work count.',['§3.6 The placement rule','§3.2 (1)'],'kernel',['@queries','@routeEdge'],['@insertChain steps 2-4','@splitThrough','@transientArena input sides','@warp re-seat','W_PLACED'],['placement','placement rule','J-K17','hot pixel'])
it('KERNEL-0247','rule','3.6','Placement rule behaviour: the nearest lattice point qualifies almost always; it fails only where a vertex already stands between minEdge and minEdge + 0.71 cm from the edge (a gap a previous rounding or a warp can leave) and a point rounded towards it would bring it inside minEdge; then a qualifying point lies a few cm along the edge (a point about 5 cm along the hot-pixel band is at least minEdge from it). Measured (fixall_kernel_r2_attract.out C): 200 000 crossings next to a vertex 25-26 cm from the crossed edge - plain nearest rounding leaves 1 361 edges shorter than minEdge; the placement rule leaves none, moves a crossing at most 0.84 cm and keeps it within 0.71 cm of the crossed edge.',['§3.6 The placement rule'],'kernel',['@placement'],['§13.1 placement fixtures'],['placement receipt'])
it('KERNEL-0248','fallback','3.6','Placement rule fallback: if no candidate qualifies, the incoming vertex moves onto the nearer endpoint of the edge (a crossing routes its segment through that endpoint) and the new segments re-enter step 3; each re-route lands on an existing vertex, so the loop ends within the work limit.',['§3.6 The placement rule'],'kernel',['@placement'],['@insertChain step 3','@workLimit'],['no-candidate fallback'])
it('KERNEL-0249','rule','3.6','W_PLACED reports every vertex the placement rule places away from its nearest lattice point: a new, re-seated or sliding vertex seated at a qualifying non-nearest point, and an incoming vertex moved onto the edge endpoint because no candidate qualified. Snap rounding is the same rule for points on no existing edge (nearest lattice point).',['§3.6 The placement rule','§3.1 Warning comment'],'kernel',['@placement'],['W_PLACED','§5.4 report'],['W_PLACED semantics'])
it('KERNEL-0250','rule','3.6','Placement bound drift (J-K32): the rule gives every vertex it places, re-seats or slides onto a coarser edge §3.10 placement bound; a later same-level change of that route (a split, promotion, demotion or dissolution, §2.2 rule 3) moves no finer vertex and may leave one farther off: one promotion in ten leaves another finer vertex up to about 1.25 cm from its new route edge, a same-level split about as often, and successive changes up to about 1.36 cm after two and about 1.49 cm after three or four (fixall_kernel_r4_checks.out A, fixall_kernel_r5_checks.out B). Nothing reads that standing distance but the index, whose route-edge boxes cover it (§3.3).',['§3.6 after placement rule','§3.3 route-edge box'],'kernel',['@placement'],['@routeEdge box','@invariants placement bound'],['placement bound','J-K32','1.25 / 1.36 / 1.49 cm'])
it('KERNEL-0251','rule','3.6','Existing geometry wins: an incoming chain vertex moves at most sqrt(2) * attractCm = about 36.8 cm (onto an edge endpoint from beside the edge), or by the step-4 corner bound at an acute tip; no existing vertex moves; an existing edge moves only by its hot pixel (<= 0.71 cm) where a vertex is placed on it.',['§3.6 Existing geometry wins'],'kernel',['@attraction','@placement'],['@invariants'],['existing geometry wins','36.8 cm'])
it('KERNEL-0252','rule','3.6','No edge shorter than minEdge can be created: every edge a batch creates is one of four kinds, each >= minEdge: (a) a chain segment between consecutive chain vertices (decimation and merge rule); (b) a sub-edge of a split existing edge (placement rule (ii)); (c) a chain segment ending at a split point or crossing ((ii) again for a crossing segment own endpoints, and the merge rule when the chain previous vertex is an existing vertex beside the split); (d) the span between two crossings (resolved at the acute corner by step 4). Clearance holds by steps 2-3 for every new vertex and segment and by (iii) for every placed vertex.',['§3.6 Existing geometry wins'],'kernel',['@insertChain','@placement'],['@invariants minEdge, clearance','GEO-35'],['four edge kinds'])
it('KERNEL-0253','receipt','3.6','Measured (fixall_kernel_r2_attract.out A-B): over 400 000 vertices dropped in the attraction band of random lattice edges, 0 sub-edges shorter than minEdge (shortest 25.50 cm; 5 729 moved onto an endpoint; largest displacement 36.72 cm); on cut endpoints of Chaikin x2 spines with 7.9 m median segments (fixall_kernel_r2_attract.py B), 6.38 % attracted, displaced p50 12.9 cm and at most 26 cm, shortest resulting edge 25.6 cm. Constructive fixtures: §13.1.',['§3.6 Existing geometry wins'],'kernel',['@attraction'],['§13.1 constructive fixtures'],['attraction receipt'])
